      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the standard implications of a force majeure clause in a Singapore commercial lease, specifically regarding how it impacts a retail tenant's obligation to pay rent during an extended period of business interruption?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, a force majeure clause in a commercial lease may exempt a retail tenant from paying rent if a rent-free period for business interruption from beyond their control is agreed upon in the contract, but doesn't extend indefinitely and clearly defines the circumstances. Tenants cannot generally withhold rent indefinitely; they may need to apply for rent relief or compensation specifically granted by state-level initiatives or bankruptcy proceedings. It is important for both landlords and tenants to consult their commercial lease agreement or local laws to understand the exact obligations during such events.",
