    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it really comes down to what was realistic to expect at the time of signing. what about the \"beyond control\" aspect? if there's a chaotic or widespread disruption that makes operations impossible to manage, how do they distinguish between a legitimate external event and just bad luck or poor management by the tenant?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under Singapore law, establishing an event as \"beyond control\" involves demonstrating that the unforeseen event or circumstance has rendered performance physically or legally impossible. Legally imposed factors, such as government-imposed lockdowns generally typify this. For disruptions resulting from supply chain issues, plaintiffs need to demonstrate that despite the event, equivalent alternative measures or actions couldn't have reasonably been taken by the defendant.\n\n\nAlternatively, court analyses may include questioning whether proper planning or risk assessment was in effect prior to the event. If the tenant failed adequately to foresee potential disruptions or to put processes that could handle disruptions proactively in place, this may constitute poor management rather than an inability to perform the contractual obligations.\n\n\nThe defendant would face difficulty in claiming that an event was beyond their control if the logistics, financial, or contractual arrangements could have been adjusted to mitigate the impacts reasonably anticipated. The distinction generally lies in demonstrating that the incident negates the possibility of performance under expected conditions versus the failure to adopt reasonable contingency and risk mitigation measures.",
      "source": "target",
